# =============================================================================
# gitleaks per-finding suppressions (security.yml job `gitleaks`).
#
# One line per audited finding, NEVER a path and NEVER a rule — a new secret in
# any of these files still fails the scan. Entries appear in two forms because
# the fingerprint depends on the scan mode: `<commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line>`
# when gitleaks walks git history (how CI runs it, fetch-depth 0) and
# `<file>:<rule>:<line>` for a working-tree scan (`gitleaks detect --no-git`).
# The two disagree on line numbers when a file changed after the commit that
# introduced the finding; both are listed.
#
# Audit performed 2026-09-08 over the full history (109 commits). Verdict for
# all of them: none is a credential. Most are string literals inside
# `#[cfg(test)]` modules, two are doc comments naming a PEM header, and two are
# unparseable placeholder JWTs. The RSA keys in changelly.rs were generated for
# the test suite and authenticate nothing — the real Changelly key is
# operator-installed through /admin/keys and never lives in the repo
# (CLAUDE.md § Key custody).
#
# NOTE: comments here deliberately avoid writing PEM header lines verbatim,
# because this file is itself scanned.
#
# Re-audit trigger: any NEW fingerprint reported by the job — including one of
# these findings reappearing under a later commit because its line moved. Do
# not add a line here without reading the flagged code and recording why it is
# not a secret.
# =============================================================================

